          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Tired all the time for a couple of months, and my periods have been heavy.",
          "answer": "**Urgency: routine.** The red-flag check found no emergency pattern in the rules it covers. That is not a clearance. A symptom not on the list can still be serious, so get medical advice soon, and sooner if things change.\n\n**Seek urgent care now if you notice any of these** (from the gastrointestinal bleeding and breathing guidance):\n- Dizziness or faintness, especially when you stand up.\n- A racing or pounding heartbeat, pale skin, or sweating.\n- Breathlessness at rest, or breathlessness that starts suddenly.\n- Chest pain, or chest pain together with breathlessness.\n- Very heavy bleeding, such as soaking a pad or more every hour for several hours, or passing large clots.\n- Black or bloody stools, or vomiting blood.\n\nBlood loss can cause serious symptoms before much blood is visible, so these signs matter more than how much you see.\n\n**What the guidance says about these symptoms.** Fatigue together with heavy periods is a combination that clinicians commonly ask about. The iron-deficiency anemia summary describes fatigue, breathlessness on exertion, and dizziness as typical features, and says a blood count and ferritin test are the usual first checks. Its onset is often gradual, which is why people may not notice it until it is well established. The hypothyroidism summary lists both fatigue and heavy or irregular periods among its symptoms, and says it is confirmed with repeated blood tests. The type 2 diabetes summary lists fatigue as a possible symptom too. These are conditions to raise with a clinician, not conclusions about you.\n\n**What to do next.** Book a routine appointment soon and mention the two-month timeline and the heavy periods. Your clinician may want a blood count and other blood tests, and may ask about your cycle, bleeding amount, diet, sleep, and medications. Bring a note of when the tiredness and bleeding started and how they have changed.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
